WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.325 --> 00:00:03.726
[suspenseful music]

00:00:03.726 --> 00:00:05.021
[witch growling loudly]

00:00:05.021 --> 00:00:05.854
[woman screams]

00:00:05.854 --> 00:00:07.630
Narrator: This is the reveal of the witch

00:00:07.630 --> 00:00:10.080
in 2013's "The Conjuring."

00:00:10.080 --> 00:00:12.630
Compare that to this reveal of Jason

00:00:12.630 --> 00:00:14.853
in "Jason Takes Manhattan."

00:00:14.853 --> 00:00:16.350
[suspenseful music]

00:00:16.350 --> 00:00:18.760
The first one is much scarier,

00:00:18.760 --> 00:00:20.450
but don't just take my word for it.

00:00:20.450 --> 00:00:22.800
Jason has certainly had his scary moments,

00:00:22.800 --> 00:00:25.460
but one of the main reasons
"Jason Takes Manhattan"

00:00:25.460 --> 00:00:28.350
is ranked so low is because
it doesn't follow the rules

00:00:28.350 --> 00:00:30.760
for making a movie scary.

00:00:30.760 --> 00:00:32.470
And while there's no exact formula,

00:00:32.470 --> 00:00:35.060
there are certain time-tested
secrets you can include

00:00:35.060 --> 00:00:37.740
to create a truly terrifying jump scare,

00:00:37.740 --> 00:00:38.810
according to this woman...

00:00:38.810 --> 00:00:40.568
Rebekah McKendry: My students kind of

00:00:40.568 --> 00:00:42.867
jokingly know me as Dr. Horror.

00:00:42.867 --> 00:00:44.940
Narrator: Who has a PhD in horror.

00:00:44.940 --> 00:00:48.270
McKendry: So, jump scares
are based on kind of

00:00:48.270 --> 00:00:50.720
a theory of what fear is.

00:00:50.720 --> 00:00:53.050
Fear is kind of the buildup.
[water running]

00:00:53.050 --> 00:00:54.962
It's the time in between

00:00:54.962 --> 00:00:57.436
when you think something might happen

00:00:57.436 --> 00:00:59.311
to when it actually happened.

00:00:59.311 --> 00:01:00.630
[woman screaming]

00:01:00.630 --> 00:01:02.190
Narrator: You can usually
tell when a jump scare

00:01:02.190 --> 00:01:04.290
is about to happen. The lighting dims,

00:01:04.290 --> 00:01:05.273
[lights shut off]

00:01:05.273 --> 00:01:07.225
the editing slows down,

00:01:07.225 --> 00:01:10.140
things might start moving by themselves,

00:01:10.140 --> 00:01:13.023
and the music gets, well, creepy.

00:01:13.023 --> 00:01:16.023
[suspenseful music]

00:01:20.470 --> 00:01:23.210
But those are the more obvious clues.

00:01:23.210 --> 00:01:24.793
So, what is the first thing

00:01:24.793 --> 00:01:27.101
that helps build to a jump scare?

00:01:27.101 --> 00:01:28.312
McKendry: I call it breadcrumbs.

00:01:28.312 --> 00:01:30.970
You get some type of indication

00:01:30.970 --> 00:01:33.550
that something scary
may be likely to happen.

00:01:33.550 --> 00:01:35.700
The closet door opens slightly.

00:01:35.700 --> 00:01:37.010
We see a window open.

00:01:37.010 --> 00:01:39.080
There's a weird noise
coming from the corner.

00:01:39.080 --> 00:01:41.410
Narrator: There are a few
famous audio examples of this

00:01:41.410 --> 00:01:42.860
from classic horror.

00:01:42.860 --> 00:01:44.551
In the "Friday the 13th" series,

00:01:44.551 --> 00:01:46.309
you know something bad is about to happen

00:01:46.309 --> 00:01:47.941
when you hear Jason's...

00:01:47.941 --> 00:01:51.350
Jason: Ki ki ki ma ma ma ki ki...

00:01:51.350 --> 00:01:52.682
Narrator: Or when you hear the famous

00:01:52.682 --> 00:01:56.674
[suspenseful music]

00:01:56.674 --> 00:01:57.724
in "Jaws."

00:01:57.724 --> 00:01:59.530
But the breadcrumbs we're talking about

00:01:59.530 --> 00:02:00.890
are often more subtle,

00:02:00.890 --> 00:02:03.950
like in this scene from
the 2002 film "Below."

00:02:03.950 --> 00:02:07.240
The character's reflection
starts to lag in the mirror,

00:02:07.240 --> 00:02:09.370
so you begin to get that
feeling that something

00:02:09.370 --> 00:02:12.530
is very wrong and that
something even more scary

00:02:12.530 --> 00:02:13.580
is about to happen.

00:02:13.580 --> 00:02:16.070
Now let's take a look back
at that wardrobe scene

00:02:16.070 --> 00:02:17.150
in "The Conjuring."

00:02:17.150 --> 00:02:18.970
There are several breadcrumbs that lead us

00:02:18.970 --> 00:02:20.580
to the final witch scare.

00:02:20.580 --> 00:02:23.330
We first have the sound
of a closet door banging.

00:02:23.330 --> 00:02:24.163
[closet door bangs]

00:02:24.163 --> 00:02:26.657
Then the visual of the
girl walking in her sleep.

00:02:26.657 --> 00:02:27.990
[closet door bangs]
Andrea: Cindy?

00:02:27.990 --> 00:02:29.880
Narrator: Notice the camera hardly moves

00:02:29.880 --> 00:02:32.590
and the wardrobe never
leaves the wide shot.

00:02:32.590 --> 00:02:35.250
And then we get the sound
of even more banging.

00:02:35.250 --> 00:02:37.600
Only this time, the
girl is back in her bed,

00:02:37.600 --> 00:02:39.932
so we know it's not her anymore.

00:02:39.932 --> 00:02:41.814
[closet door bangs]

00:02:41.814 --> 00:02:42.647
[closet banging continues]

00:02:42.647 --> 00:02:44.056
Cue the creepy music.

00:02:44.056 --> 00:02:45.280
[suspenseful music]

00:02:45.280 --> 00:02:47.832
Each breadcrumb raises
our level of anxiety

00:02:47.832 --> 00:02:49.173
a little bit more,

00:02:49.173 --> 00:02:52.040
like a balloon gradually
getting more inflated

00:02:52.040 --> 00:02:53.996
until it finally pops.

00:02:53.996 --> 00:02:55.121
[woman gasps]

00:02:55.121 --> 00:02:57.526
[woman breathing shakily]

00:02:57.526 --> 00:02:59.041
[witch growling loudly]

00:02:59.041 --> 00:03:00.010
[woman screams]

00:03:00.010 --> 00:03:01.410
There are no specific breadcrumbs

00:03:01.410 --> 00:03:03.228
in the "Jason Takes Manhattan" scene.

00:03:03.228 --> 00:03:05.550
And, in fact, we don't
even hear the classic

00:03:05.550 --> 00:03:09.000
"ki ki ki ma ma ma"
until after we know Jason

00:03:09.000 --> 00:03:10.892
is already right behind them.

00:03:10.892 --> 00:03:12.900
Jason: Ki ki ki ki ki ki...

00:03:12.900 --> 00:03:14.550
Narrator: Once we see
and hear these clues,

00:03:14.550 --> 00:03:16.790
that doesn't mean the scare
is gonna come right away.

00:03:16.790 --> 00:03:19.365
In fact, for most great jump scares,

00:03:19.365 --> 00:03:20.610
the opposite is true.

00:03:20.610 --> 00:03:22.493
You have to make the viewer wait

00:03:22.493 --> 00:03:23.793
by dragging out the scene

00:03:23.793 --> 00:03:26.532
until the tension is too much to bear.

00:03:28.370 --> 00:03:30.770
Alfred Hitchcock famously said:

00:03:34.430 --> 00:03:36.250
McKendry: Once the gun
is fired, [gun fires]

00:03:36.250 --> 00:03:38.430
the fear is done. It's over with.

00:03:38.430 --> 00:03:40.150
There's nothing left to be scared of.

00:03:40.150 --> 00:03:42.880
Once the gun is fired, the scene is over.

00:03:42.880 --> 00:03:45.100
Narrator: An effective
jump scare can take time.

00:03:45.100 --> 00:03:47.810
Even the majority of the
movie, in some cases.

00:03:47.810 --> 00:03:50.580
But if done right, the
payoff will be well worth it.

00:03:50.580 --> 00:03:52.230
One great example of buildup

00:03:52.230 --> 00:03:53.710
is this nurse-station scene

00:03:53.710 --> 00:03:55.050
in "The Exorcist III."

00:03:55.050 --> 00:03:58.050
The camera doesn't move at
all for about three minutes

00:03:58.050 --> 00:04:00.710
while nurses and security
guards shuffle around.

00:04:00.710 --> 00:04:02.940
We know something is about to happen.

00:04:02.940 --> 00:04:04.340
We just don't know when,

00:04:04.340 --> 00:04:06.290
and it takes its time to get there.

00:04:06.290 --> 00:04:09.740
[suspenseful music]

00:04:09.740 --> 00:04:11.575
This scene in "Insidious" is another

00:04:11.575 --> 00:04:13.200
great example of building tension.

00:04:13.200 --> 00:04:14.733
Only instead of a single shot,

00:04:14.733 --> 00:04:16.220
we alternate back and forth

00:04:16.220 --> 00:04:17.395
between two characters

00:04:17.395 --> 00:04:19.153
for an extended period of time.

00:04:19.153 --> 00:04:21.410
The conversation goes
on for about two minutes

00:04:21.410 --> 00:04:23.540
before the demon finally appears.

00:04:23.540 --> 00:04:25.830
And even though we know
something is about to happen,

00:04:25.830 --> 00:04:27.752
when it does, we're caught off guard.

00:04:27.752 --> 00:04:28.757
[demon growling]

00:04:28.757 --> 00:04:30.010
[woman screams]

00:04:30.010 --> 00:04:31.743
The wardrobe scene in "The Conjuring"

00:04:31.743 --> 00:04:33.751
takes place over about two minutes

00:04:33.751 --> 00:04:35.280
after the first breadcrumb.

00:04:35.280 --> 00:04:38.700
Director James Wan lets the
scene play out very slowly,

00:04:38.700 --> 00:04:40.640
with a creeping sense of dread.

00:04:40.640 --> 00:04:43.740
The scene in "Jason Takes
Manhattan" is also long,

00:04:43.740 --> 00:04:45.130
about two minutes.

00:04:45.130 --> 00:04:47.820
But Jason is revealed
so early on in the scene

00:04:47.820 --> 00:04:50.840
with no buildup about one
minute after it starts.

00:04:50.840 --> 00:04:52.593
So the only thing we're waiting for

00:04:52.593 --> 00:04:55.070
is for the couple to see
him 30 seconds later.

00:04:55.070 --> 00:04:59.210
For us as the audience,
there's no tension, no anxiety.

00:04:59.210 --> 00:05:00.760
It's during this period of buildup

00:05:00.760 --> 00:05:03.380
when the best horror movies
will use a distraction

00:05:03.380 --> 00:05:05.095
to further amp up the tension.

00:05:05.095 --> 00:05:07.427
Lars: Boo!
Derek: Jesus Christ.

00:05:07.427 --> 00:05:08.311
[beep]

00:05:10.230 --> 00:05:12.330
Narrator: Think of this
part like a magic trick.

00:05:12.330 --> 00:05:14.150
You're encouraged to look in one direction

00:05:14.150 --> 00:05:16.714
while the real threat
is happening elsewhere.

00:05:16.714 --> 00:05:18.740
[woman screams]

00:05:18.740 --> 00:05:20.520
In this famous scene in "Jaws,"

00:05:20.520 --> 00:05:23.060
we're distracted by the
discovery of a tooth,

00:05:23.060 --> 00:05:24.870
and we're expecting to see a shark.

00:05:24.870 --> 00:05:26.822
Instead, we get a corpse.

00:05:26.822 --> 00:05:28.320
[woman screams]

00:05:28.320 --> 00:05:30.980
In "The Sixth Sense" when
Cole and the audience

00:05:30.980 --> 00:05:32.690
is focusing on the closet,

00:05:32.690 --> 00:05:35.855
an arm shoots out from under the bed.

00:05:35.855 --> 00:05:37.370
[Cole gasps]

00:05:37.370 --> 00:05:40.460
One common and often
overused form of misdirection

00:05:40.460 --> 00:05:41.672
is the mirror trope.

00:05:41.672 --> 00:05:42.505
[man screams]

00:05:42.505 --> 00:05:43.738
Where the protagonist opens

00:05:43.738 --> 00:05:45.570
a medicine cabinet, for example.

00:05:45.570 --> 00:05:47.510
Alone at first, when it shuts,

00:05:47.510 --> 00:05:49.685
something is standing behind them.

00:05:49.685 --> 00:05:50.518
Surprise!

00:05:50.518 --> 00:05:51.520
[woman gasps]

00:05:51.520 --> 00:05:54.100
The technique was first
used in Roman Polanski's

00:05:54.100 --> 00:05:56.630
1965 film "Repulsion"

00:05:56.630 --> 00:05:59.782
and can be found in numerous
other films to this day.

00:05:59.782 --> 00:06:03.510
[suspenseful music]
[woman gasps]

00:06:03.510 --> 00:06:05.230
In "The Conjuring," both the camera

00:06:05.230 --> 00:06:07.410
and girl are focused on the closet.

00:06:07.410 --> 00:06:10.060
We think something is inside
of it, ready to jump out.

00:06:10.060 --> 00:06:12.090
But in reality, the threat is actually

00:06:12.090 --> 00:06:13.497
on top of the wardrobe.

00:06:13.497 --> 00:06:15.722
"Jason Takes Manhattan" doesn't even try

00:06:15.722 --> 00:06:16.797
to fool us at all.

00:06:16.797 --> 00:06:19.845
The main characters have
a false sense of security.

00:06:19.845 --> 00:06:21.280
Sean: It's over.
[cars honking]

00:06:21.280 --> 00:06:22.550
Narrator: But we as the viewer

00:06:22.550 --> 00:06:25.390
already know where the
threat is the entire time.

00:06:25.390 --> 00:06:28.150
This brings us to another
visual trick with jump scares.

00:06:28.150 --> 00:06:30.030
Making the audience feel more anxious

00:06:30.030 --> 00:06:31.662
by limiting what they can see.

00:06:31.662 --> 00:06:32.780
[beep]

00:06:32.780 --> 00:06:34.213
McKendry: Anything that you can do

00:06:34.213 --> 00:06:37.571
to limit the character, and through that,

00:06:37.571 --> 00:06:40.150
the audience's frame of vision,

00:06:40.150 --> 00:06:44.410
will always increase the
anxiety and the scare of it.

00:06:44.410 --> 00:06:46.340
Narrator: There are two
ways of accomplishing this,

00:06:46.340 --> 00:06:47.790
camera and lighting.

00:06:47.790 --> 00:06:49.440
Let's take a look at lighting first.

00:06:49.440 --> 00:06:51.690
Some common tricks are
to use a camera flash,

00:06:51.690 --> 00:06:53.380
like this scene in "Mama,"

00:06:53.380 --> 00:06:55.940
or a device like night-vision
goggles in "The Descent"

00:06:55.940 --> 00:06:59.240
to narrow the character's
and our scope of vision.

00:06:59.240 --> 00:07:02.520
Notice how much of the scene
is obscured in these shots.

00:07:02.520 --> 00:07:05.240
We have very little space to feel safe.

00:07:05.240 --> 00:07:06.533
McKendry: All of these things have

00:07:06.533 --> 00:07:08.590
what we call in lighting a hard falloff,

00:07:08.590 --> 00:07:10.263
meaning there is a very sharp line

00:07:10.263 --> 00:07:13.060
between what you can see
and what you can't see.

00:07:13.060 --> 00:07:14.890
Narrator: That being
said, you can still have

00:07:14.890 --> 00:07:16.460
jump scares during the day.

00:07:16.460 --> 00:07:18.518
But these require a bigger buildup,

00:07:18.518 --> 00:07:19.651
as we mentioned earlier,

00:07:19.651 --> 00:07:21.270
for them to be effective.

00:07:21.270 --> 00:07:24.230
Just look at this terrifying
moment in "Mulholland Drive."

00:07:24.230 --> 00:07:26.540
We don't see the creature
outside the diner

00:07:26.540 --> 00:07:29.440
until after an entire
three-minute-long buildup

00:07:29.440 --> 00:07:32.048
of conversation inside the diner.

00:07:32.048 --> 00:07:33.584
[air whooshing]

00:07:35.017 --> 00:07:37.180
"The Conjuring" limited
how much we can see

00:07:37.180 --> 00:07:39.690
by having only one small lamp by the bed

00:07:39.690 --> 00:07:43.230
in an otherwise dark room
far away from the wardrobe.

00:07:43.230 --> 00:07:44.928
Jason, on the other hand,

00:07:44.928 --> 00:07:47.520
is standing in a brightly
lit Times Square,

00:07:47.520 --> 00:07:49.665
where the only time he's obscured is when

00:07:49.665 --> 00:07:51.490
two people pass in front of him.

00:07:51.490 --> 00:07:54.440
The other way to limit view
is by using the camera.

00:07:54.440 --> 00:07:56.812
McKendry: It is very common
in horror films to limit the

00:07:56.812 --> 00:07:59.150
character's kind of vision,
their frame of reference,

00:07:59.150 --> 00:08:01.070
where they're looking through keyholes.

00:08:01.070 --> 00:08:02.540
They might be trapped in a closet

00:08:02.540 --> 00:08:04.780
and they're looking out slats of a closet.

00:08:04.780 --> 00:08:07.490
And really kind of limit
how much they can see.

00:08:07.490 --> 00:08:09.440
And with that, you are limiting how much

00:08:09.440 --> 00:08:11.230
the audience can see as well.

00:08:11.230 --> 00:08:13.580
Narrator: Let's take a look at
this scene in "The Shining."

00:08:13.580 --> 00:08:15.300
The camera is following Danny,

00:08:15.300 --> 00:08:18.390
so we as the audience can't
see around the corners

00:08:18.390 --> 00:08:21.443
of the hall until he
does, when it's too late.

00:08:22.710 --> 00:08:25.300
When Arbogast is walking
up the steps in "Psycho,"

00:08:25.300 --> 00:08:27.220
the camera is focused on him,

00:08:27.220 --> 00:08:29.670
so we are also blind to what's around him

00:08:29.670 --> 00:08:31.010
and what's about to happen.

00:08:31.010 --> 00:08:34.204
It creates an added sense
of anxiety in the viewer.

00:08:34.204 --> 00:08:36.758
[suspenseful music]

00:08:36.758 --> 00:08:38.008
McKendry: The tighter you are

00:08:38.008 --> 00:08:39.308
and the more confined you are

00:08:39.308 --> 00:08:43.000
and the less information you
have about the situation,

00:08:43.000 --> 00:08:44.620
that's when it's scary.

00:08:44.620 --> 00:08:46.840
Narrator: And camera
movements can also force you

00:08:46.840 --> 00:08:49.390
into areas you wouldn't
normally choose to go.

00:08:49.390 --> 00:08:51.020
McKendry: If the closet is slightly open,

00:08:51.020 --> 00:08:54.370
the camera may start pushing
into the darkness slightly.

00:08:54.370 --> 00:08:58.140
The filmmaker is forcing
you as the audience

00:08:58.140 --> 00:09:00.650
to get closer to it,
whether you want to or not.

00:09:00.650 --> 00:09:03.630
Or, sometimes, the camera
will hold perfectly still.

00:09:03.630 --> 00:09:05.520
And that in itself can be equally anxious,

00:09:05.520 --> 00:09:08.280
'cause we're used to movement
and cuts and things like that.

00:09:08.280 --> 00:09:10.470
Narrator: And when a
scare finally appears,

00:09:10.470 --> 00:09:12.995
it's typically accompanied
by a loud sound.

00:09:12.995 --> 00:09:14.001
[air whooshing]

00:09:14.001 --> 00:09:16.182
This sound is known as a sting.

00:09:16.182 --> 00:09:17.022
[beep]

00:09:17.980 --> 00:09:20.380
A good example of a sting
can actually be heard

00:09:20.380 --> 00:09:22.380
in the opening credits of "Insidious,"

00:09:22.380 --> 00:09:25.460
a film that is full of
effective jump scares.

00:09:25.460 --> 00:09:27.300
The crash accentuates the moment

00:09:27.300 --> 00:09:29.079
we are supposed to be scared.

00:09:29.079 --> 00:09:31.010
[screeching music]

00:09:31.010 --> 00:09:33.050
There's certainly a sting
when the witch appears

00:09:33.050 --> 00:09:35.951
in "The Conjuring," accompanied
by a camera push-in.

00:09:35.951 --> 00:09:37.190
[witch growling loudly]

00:09:37.190 --> 00:09:39.819
And there's actually two
in "Jason Takes Manhattan."

00:09:39.819 --> 00:09:42.219
[air whooshing loudly]
Jason: Ki ki ki ki...

00:09:42.219 --> 00:09:43.644
[screeching music]

00:09:43.644 --> 00:09:45.180
Narrator: But these are both ineffective,

00:09:45.180 --> 00:09:46.970
since we've already seen Jason.

00:09:46.970 --> 00:09:48.910
There's no peak moment of anxiety

00:09:48.910 --> 00:09:51.010
that is elevated by the sound.

00:09:51.010 --> 00:09:54.300
A sting can only work so
well without a good buildup.

00:09:54.300 --> 00:09:56.690
There are numerous examples
of this in horror films,

00:09:56.690 --> 00:09:58.480
where the jump scare and the loud sting

00:09:58.480 --> 00:10:00.460
come completely out of nowhere.

00:10:00.460 --> 00:10:02.200
Sure, you may be startled,

00:10:02.200 --> 00:10:04.027
but this fear won't last.
[woman screams]

00:10:04.027 --> 00:10:07.070
"Deep Blue Sea" has a memorable
jump scare with a sting,

00:10:07.070 --> 00:10:09.360
but there's no breadcrumb and no buildup.

00:10:09.360 --> 00:10:12.550
And so while the scene is
certainly violent and unexpected,

00:10:12.550 --> 00:10:15.270
there's also no real fear
after the shark eats him.

00:10:15.270 --> 00:10:16.730
And the absurdity of it all

00:10:16.730 --> 00:10:18.971
might just make you laugh instead.

00:10:18.971 --> 00:10:20.900
[shark growling]
[women screaming]

00:10:20.900 --> 00:10:23.210
And after all of the work
to build up the scare,

00:10:23.210 --> 00:10:25.003
the threat needs to hold up as well.

00:10:25.003 --> 00:10:27.197
[wall clatters]
[woman gasps]

00:10:27.197 --> 00:10:28.038
[beep]

00:10:29.180 --> 00:10:31.520
In a lot of movies, the threat isn't real,

00:10:31.520 --> 00:10:32.959
but it can still make you jump.

00:10:32.959 --> 00:10:34.236
[woman screams]

00:10:34.236 --> 00:10:35.069
[woman exhales deeply]

00:10:35.069 --> 00:10:36.740
McKendry: Oh, my gosh,
the closet door's opening,

00:10:36.740 --> 00:10:38.882
we're getting closer, closer, closer,

00:10:38.882 --> 00:10:40.140
the music's swelling,

00:10:40.140 --> 00:10:41.623
the camera's pushing in,

00:10:41.623 --> 00:10:42.714
and then it's a cat.

00:10:42.714 --> 00:10:44.010
[cat growling]
[woman screams]

00:10:44.010 --> 00:10:46.890
Cats have had more jump
scares in horror history

00:10:46.890 --> 00:10:48.930
than any monster ever had.

00:10:48.930 --> 00:10:50.480
Narrator: Like in "The Amityville Horror"

00:10:50.480 --> 00:10:52.996
when one just appears outside the window.

00:10:52.996 --> 00:10:55.970
[cat growling]

00:10:55.970 --> 00:10:58.286
One of the most famous fake scares --

00:10:58.286 --> 00:10:59.844
it's actually not a cat --

00:10:59.844 --> 00:11:03.974
was this bus in the
1942 film "Cat People."

00:11:03.974 --> 00:11:06.820
[bus whooshing loudly]

00:11:06.820 --> 00:11:08.497
In fact, this scene went on to inspire

00:11:08.497 --> 00:11:10.720
other filmmakers to mimic the fake scare

00:11:10.720 --> 00:11:13.240
and has become known as
"the Lewton Bus technique,"

00:11:13.240 --> 00:11:15.630
named after the film's
producer, Val Lewton.

00:11:15.630 --> 00:11:17.130
Another famous example of this

00:11:17.130 --> 00:11:19.731
is the Pop-Tart scene in "Silent Hill."

00:11:19.731 --> 00:11:20.965
[toaster pops]

00:11:22.040 --> 00:11:23.490
While it might make you jump,

00:11:23.490 --> 00:11:25.698
there's nothing terrifying
about a Pop-Tart

00:11:25.698 --> 00:11:27.520
unless you have a gluten allergy.

00:11:27.520 --> 00:11:30.620
Horror movies are littered with
these types of cheap scares,

00:11:30.620 --> 00:11:32.790
which ultimately hurt the movie as a whole

00:11:32.790 --> 00:11:34.570
because they haven't been earned

00:11:34.570 --> 00:11:36.648
and don't move the story forward.

00:11:36.648 --> 00:11:38.030
[woman screams]

00:11:38.030 --> 00:11:41.980
The best way to use a jump
scare is to not overuse it.

00:11:41.980 --> 00:11:44.780
Too many jump scares takes
away from the impact of them.

00:11:44.780 --> 00:11:46.986
And sometimes you don't
even need them at all.

00:11:46.986 --> 00:11:47.819
[man growling]
[woman screaming]

00:11:47.819 --> 00:11:50.440
In fact, best-picture winner
"The Silence of the Lambs"

00:11:50.440 --> 00:11:53.480
doesn't even have one jump
scare in the entire movie.

00:11:53.480 --> 00:11:57.010
And the classic "Texas Chain
Saw Massacre" only has two.

00:11:57.010 --> 00:11:59.608
McKendry: If you're just
up here the whole time,

00:11:59.608 --> 00:12:01.910
you go flat. There's nowhere else to go.

00:12:01.910 --> 00:12:04.960
And so it's all about kind
of taking the audience

00:12:04.960 --> 00:12:07.740
on a roller-coaster
ride of hills and loops,

00:12:07.740 --> 00:12:09.830
but not trying to keep
them suspended in the air

00:12:09.830 --> 00:12:10.880
the entire time.

00:12:10.880 --> 00:12:13.140
Narrator: And when a jump
scare does work well,

00:12:13.140 --> 00:12:15.123
you'll know, because you'll see it pop up

00:12:15.123 --> 00:12:16.514
again and again,

00:12:16.514 --> 00:12:18.680
replicated in newer films as a tribute

00:12:18.680 --> 00:12:20.220
to its original success

00:12:20.220 --> 00:12:22.421
and the work that went in to crafting it.

00:12:22.421 --> 00:12:24.738
[creature growling]

00:12:24.738 --> 00:12:26.293
[woman screams]

00:12:26.293 --> 00:12:27.629
[screaming intensifies]

00:12:27.629 --> 00:12:30.629
[suspenseful music]

